     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** The usual adult dose of sertraline is 50\u2013200 mg once daily, and 200 mg per day is the maximum. 1000 mg is five times that limit. At that dose there's a real risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures, heart rhythm problems (QT prolongation) and other serious toxicity. The patient's normal kidney and liver function doesn't make this dose safe.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a clinician with prescribing authority.** This system is set up for licensed prescribers to enter medication orders. Since you're not a clinician, I can't enter one for you, whatever your role at the hospital. This rule protects patients, and it protects you and the hospital from liability.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- If this patient needs sertraline, please ask the attending physician or another prescriber on the medicine team to review and enter the order. They would typically start at 50 mg once daily.\n- If \"1000 mg\" came from a written note or a verbal request, it may be a transcription error, such as an extra zero on 100 mg. The prescriber and pharmacy should clarify it before anything is given.\n- If you think a dose like this may already have been ordered or given, contact the ward pharmacist and the patient's care team right away.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms an appropriate order, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
