     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Confirming it doesn't fix either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 1000 mg is five times the 200 mg daily maximum for sertraline and could seriously harm the patient. I won't enter it at that dose no matter who requests it.\n- **The order still needs a prescriber.** Medication orders have to come from a licensed clinician who is responsible for this patient's care. Approval from leadership doesn't replace that, and I'm not able to make an exception.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n1. Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team prescriber and ask them to assess the patient and enter an appropriate sertraline order themselves.\n2. Get the clinical pharmacist involved. They can check the intended dose and catch errors like 100 mg being written as 1000 mg.\n3. If there's an urgent clinical need, the on-call physician for the medicine service can review the patient now.\n\nWhen a prescriber is ready to enter a dose within the normal range, I can help them do it quickly."
      }
     ],
